Gate the catalog colour guards, which ran in every application and hid the caller.

The two respondsToSelector guards from 0040 had no switch. They send a message on every catalog
colour construction and every conversion, which is a hot path while anything is drawing, and a
guard that messages a freed entry FAULTS AT THE GUARD: every core for task #227 named my line and
the selector resolved to respondsToSelector:, so the application own send was invisible.

With them behind CIDER_TRACE_SYSCOLOR the same crash names -[NSColor_catalog
colorUsingColorSpaceName:device:] sending colorUsingColorSpaceName:device: to _color, which is
what identified the dead object as System controlTextColor.

The gate is cached because a getenv per colour conversion would be the same hot path problem in
another form.
--- a/AppKit/NSColor.subproj/NSColor_catalog.m
+++ b/AppKit/NSColor.subproj/NSColor_catalog.m
@@ -123,6 +123,16 @@
     return NULL;
 }
 
+/* The two guards below must stay off by default: each MESSAGES the entry it is checking, so against
+ * a freed one it faults AT THE GUARD and every core for #227 named it, not the application own send.
+ * Cached because a getenv per colour conversion is itself a hot path. */
+static BOOL _CiderSysColorTracing(void) {
+    static int cached = -1;
+    if (cached < 0)
+        cached = (getenv("CIDER_TRACE_SYSCOLOR") != NULL) ? 1 : 0;
+    return cached != 0;
+}
+
 @implementation NSColor_catalog
 
 - initWithCatalogName: (NSColorListName) catalogName
@@ -162,7 +172,8 @@
      * lookup does. If this never fires and the one in colorUsingColorSpaceName:device: does, the
      * entry was good when stored and something freed it afterwards.
      */
-    if (_color != nil && ![_color respondsToSelector: @selector(colorUsingColorSpaceName:device:)]) {
+    if (_CiderSysColorTracing() && _color != nil
+        && ![_color respondsToSelector: @selector(colorUsingColorSpaceName:device:)]) {
         fprintf(stderr, "CIDER_SYSCOLOR BAD ON ARRIVAL name=%s entry=%p class=%s\n",
                 colorName != nil ? [colorName UTF8String] : "(nil)", _color,
                 object_getClassName(_color));
@@ -342,7 +353,7 @@
      * while the entry is sound, and the ONLY run that writes anything is the one that was going to
      * die two lines later, which is the run whose name nobody could read.
      */
